# Bind the UPS HAT (E) driver to the MCU at 0x2d on the 40-pin header.
#
# The Raspberry Pi OS way to do this is a device-tree overlay, and every guide
# you will find says to drop a .dtbo in /boot/firmware/overlays/ and add a
# dtoverlay= line to config.txt. On Ubuntu that directory does not exist. The
# boot partition here is laid out for tryboot (config.txt sets os_prefix=current/)
# and flash-kernel rebuilds current/overlays/ from the kernel package on every
# kernel upgrade, so anything hand-placed there is deleted the first time a new
# linux-raspi lands. ups-hat-e.dts documents the overlay route and the one
# directory that does persist; this rule is the route that needs no reboot and
# no boot-partition surgery at all.
#
# Instantiating an I2C device from userspace is a documented kernel interface,
# not a workaround — see Documentation/i2c/instantiating-devices.rst, "Method 4:
# Instantiate from user-space". It exists for exactly this case: a device on a
# general-purpose bus that the firmware's device tree has no reason to know about.
#
# Matching on OF_ALIAS_0 rather than KERNEL=="i2c-1" is deliberate. Linux bus
# numbering is assigned in probe order and the Pi 5 has four I2C adapters (two
# on the RP1, two on the SoC); "i2c-1" happens to be the header bus today. The
# device-tree alias i2c1 *is* the header bus by definition, so this keeps
# pointing at the right pins no matter how the adapters enumerate.
#
# Writing new_device emits a uevent carrying MODALIAS=i2c:ups-hat-e, so udev
# autoloads the module itself; there is nothing to add to /etc/modules-load.d.
# If the board at 0x2d is not a UPS HAT the driver's ID-register check fails the
# probe with -ENODEV and the client is simply left unbound.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="i2c", ENV{OF_ALIAS_0}=="i2c1", ATTR{new_device}="ups-hat-e 0x2d"
